  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1158 AM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nBefore 11am, heat indices in southeastern Minnesota have already \nclimbed into the upper 90s thanks to dew points pushing 80. \nFurther west, it's quite a bit cooler following last night's \nthunderstorms. Some cloud cover is also playing into this, but \nclouds will continue to clear and allow temperatures to warm \nquickly into the mid to upper 90s by early afternoon. We'll have\nno trouble seeing apparent temperatures top 100 area wide with \nreadings near or above 110 possible across southern Minnesota. \nThese temperatures and dew points will contribute to a very \nunstable airmass with CAPE values topping 3000 J/kg. However, \nmodels continue to show 700mb temps somewhere around 13 to 14C \nwhich would help to limit convection. The main focus for severe \nweather later today will likely lie across Wisconsin, where \ndamaging winds and large hail will be the primary threat. As \nmentioned in the previous discussion, if 700mb temps do end up \nbeing a degree or two cooler, we could see things evolve further\nwest across Minnesota. CAMs have pretty consistently been \nshowing storms firing in the Arrowhead and tracking into \nnorthern Wisconsin as an MCS this evening, so confidence remains\nhighest for our easternmost Wisconsin counties. SPC did \nintroduce a large enhanced risk (level 3/5) that includes pretty\nmuch all of Wisconsin with the slight risk area still extending\nback into western Minnesota. Did a morning PoP update to raise \nprecip chances (10-20%) CWA wide to keep messaging with SPC more\nconsistent.\n\nHeading into Monday, highs will be in the 90s again, but we'll\nsee winds switch around to the northwest, allowing some drier \nair to work into the region. This will help tamp down heat \nindices a little, but readings in the mid to upper 90s are still\npossible across our south. Went ahead and expanded the Heat \nAdvisory from just the Twin Cities metro to include southern \nMinnesota and portions of western Wisconsin. By Wednesday, the \naxis of the 500mb ridge will extend up through the Dakotas. A \nshortwave along the top of this ridge will move across the \neastern Dakotas/Minnesota sometime late Wednesday/early \nThursday, which brings in our next round of precip chances. The \ntiming of this feature will impact whether or not we see strong \nto severe storms on Thursday. Looking ahead, some low precip \nchances remain in place with highs in the upper 80s to start the\nmonth of August.",
